---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import SidebarNav from '../components/SidebarNav.astro';
import BottomNav from '../components/BottomNav.astro';
import MainContent from '../components/MainContent.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import PreferenceChart from '../components/PreferenceChart.astro';
import NextSteps from '../components/NextSteps.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Preference Tuning — AI Concepts Visualized"
  description="How RLHF and reward modeling align language models to human values, safety, and helpfulness."
>
  <SidebarNav activeSlug="preference-tuning" />
  <BottomNav activeTab="training" />
  <MainContent>
    <Breadcrumbs items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Training Stages', href: '/training-stages' },
      { label: 'Preference Tuning' },
    ]} />

    <!-- Hero -->
    <header class="mb-12">
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
        <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-coral" />
        <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Stage 3 of 3</span>
      </span>
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
        Preference Tuning
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose mb-4">
        Imagine training a model with a critic — someone who evaluates every output and tells the
        model whether it was good or not. That's preference tuning: the final stage that aligns a
        capable model to human values, safety, and helpfulness.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-coral">
        <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M12 2l3.09 6.26L22 9.27l-5 4.87 1.18 6.88L12 17.77l-6.18 3.25L7 14.14 2 9.27l6.91-1.01L12 2z" />
        </svg>
        <span class="font-medium">Training with a critic</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Interactive demo -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <PreferenceChart />
    </section>

    <!-- Why alignment matters -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        Why Alignment Matters
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        After SFT, a model can follow instructions well — but it can follow <em>any</em> instruction.
        Ask it to write a phishing email, and a well-trained but unaligned model will oblige.
        Preference tuning is what bridges the gap between "capable" and "helpful, harmless, and honest."
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The alignment problem isn't just about safety — it's about making the model's behavior match
        what users actually want. That means being truthful rather than confidently wrong, being
        concise rather than verbose, and pushing back when asked to do something harmful.
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-coral/20 bg-coral/6 p-5 mb-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-coral">The core idea:</strong> SFT teaches the model <em>how</em> to respond.
          Preference tuning teaches the model <em>what</em> to respond — shaping its values, not just
          its skills.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-md bg-coral/10 flex items-center justify-center">
              <span class="text-sm font-mono font-bold text-coral">1</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">Helpfulness</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
            The model learns to give answers that actually solve the user's problem — not just
            plausible-sounding text. It learns to ask clarifying questions, provide structured
            responses, and anticipate what the user needs.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-md bg-coral/10 flex items-center justify-center">
              <span class="text-sm font-mono font-bold text-coral">2</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">Honesty</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
            The model learns to admit when it doesn't know something rather than hallucinating
            an answer. It cites uncertainty, distinguishes facts from opinions, and avoids
            making up sources or statistics.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
            <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-md bg-coral/10 flex items-center justify-center">
              <span class="text-sm font-mono font-bold text-coral">3</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">Harmlessness</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
            The model learns to refuse harmful requests — from generating malware to providing
            medical advice it's not qualified for — while still being as helpful as possible
            within safe boundaries.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What is RLHF? -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        What Is RLHF?
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) is the original approach to preference
        tuning. It uses a two-stage process: first training a reward model on human preferences,
        then using that reward model to fine-tune the language model via reinforcement learning.
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">1</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Collect Preference Data</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              For each prompt, the SFT model generates multiple responses. Human annotators rank
              these responses from best to worst, creating a dataset of preference pairs that
              captures what humans actually prefer.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">2</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Train a Reward Model</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              A separate model is trained to predict human preferences. Given any prompt-response
              pair, the reward model outputs a score reflecting how much humans would like that
              response. This reward model becomes the "critic" that guides further training.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">3</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Optimize With Reinforcement Learning</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              The SFT model becomes the "policy" in a reinforcement learning setup. It generates
              responses, the reward model scores them, and the policy is updated (typically via
              PPO — Proximal Policy Optimization) to maximize reward. A KL-divergence penalty
              prevents the model from drifting too far from its SFT behavior.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- DPO as a simpler alternative -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        DPO: A Simpler Alternative
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) achieves similar results to RLHF but without the
        complexity of training a separate reward model and running reinforcement learning. Instead,
        DPO reformulates the RLHF objective as a direct loss function on preference pairs.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The key insight: the reward model in RLHF is an intermediate representation. DPO bypasses
        it entirely by deriving a closed-form policy that implicitly represents the reward function.
        This means you can optimize the model directly on preference data — one training loop instead
        of two.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-gray-200 mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-gray-50">
              <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-xs text-gray-400">
                Aspect
              </th>
              <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-medium text-coral">
                RLHF
              </th>
              <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-medium text-coral">
                DPO
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Training loops</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Two (reward model + RL)</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">One (direct optimization)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Reward model</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Trained separately</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Implicit in the policy</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Stability</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">RL can be unstable &amp; finicky</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Supervised-style training — more stable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Compute</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Higher — two models to train</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Lower — single training loop</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400">Quality</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Slightly higher (theoretically)</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-gray-600">Comparable in practice</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-coral/20 bg-coral/6 p-5">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-coral">Why DPO matters:</strong> DPO has become the dominant approach for open-weight
          models (Llama, Mistral) because it's simpler to implement, cheaper to run, and produces
          results competitive with RLHF.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The preference data pipeline -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        Building the Preference Dataset
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The quality of preference tuning depends entirely on the quality of the preference data.
        Building this dataset is one of the most labor-intensive parts of the alignment process.
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">1</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Generate Response Pairs</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              The SFT model generates 3-5 responses per prompt. Human annotators then compare these
              responses pairwise and pick the better one. This creates thousands of (chosen, rejected)
              pairs covering diverse topics and edge cases.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">2</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Cover the Distribution</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              The dataset must cover the full range of prompts the model will encounter: creative
              writing, technical questions, advice, coding, and safety edge cases. Underrepresented
              categories lead to weak alignment in those areas.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-coral/10 border border-coral/30 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-coral">3</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-0.5">Quality Control</h3>
            <p class="text-xs font-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
              Multiple annotators rate the same pairs to measure inter-rater agreement. Low-agreement
              pairs are re-reviewed or discarded. The final dataset typically contains 20K-100K
              preference pairs with strong consensus.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Key challenges -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
        Key Challenges
      </h2>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
          <span class="text-coral mr-2">⚠</span>Reward Gaming
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Models can learn to exploit flaws in the reward model — generating outputs that score
          high on the reward model but aren't actually good. This is called "reward hacking" and
          is one of the hardest problems in alignment. Techniques like adversarial training and
          reward model ensembles help mitigate this.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
          <span class="text-coral mr-2">⚠</span>Collapsing Diversity
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Aggressive preference tuning can make the model overly cautious and repetitive — always
          giving safe, bland answers. Balancing alignment with creative expressiveness requires
          careful tuning of the KL penalty and reward scaling.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
          <span class="text-coral mr-2">⚠</span>Cultural Bias
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Preference data reflects the values of the annotators who created it. If annotators come
          from a narrow demographic, the model's alignment reflects their biases. Building diverse
          annotator pools and multi-cultural preference datasets is an active area of research.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
          <span class="text-coral mr-2">⚠</span>Scaling to Frontier Models
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          As models grow more capable, human annotators struggle to evaluate their outputs.
          Comparing two brilliant pieces of writing or complex code requires expertise that
          crowdsourced annotators may not have. Self-play and AI-assisted annotation are emerging
          approaches to scale alignment for frontier models.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <NextSteps
      accentColor="var(--color-coral)"
      items={[
        { label: 'Supervised Fine-Tuning', href: '/sft', description: 'Giving the model a teacher' },
        { label: 'Tool Calling', href: '/tool-calling', description: 'Giving the agent hands to act' },
      ]}
    />
  </MainContent>
</Layout>